==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|curteis}}, from {{der|en|fro|curteis|}} (French {{m|fr|courtois}}), from {{m|fro|cort||court}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GenAm}} {{IPA|en|/ˈkɝti.əs/}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈkɜːti.əs/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-courteous.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[show#Verb|Showing]] [[regard#Noun|regard]] or [[thought#Noun|thought]] for others; especially, [[display#Verb|displaying]] good [[manners]] or [[etiquette]].
#: {{synonyms|en|Thesaurus:polite}}
#: {{antonyms|en|discourteous|uncourteous|Thesaurus:impolite}}
#: {{ux|en|a '''courteous''' gentleman &emsp; a '''courteous''' gesture}}
#* {{RQ:Keats Endymion|page=149|lines=932–935|passage=Nectar ran / In '''courteous''' fountains to all cups outreach'd; / And plunder'd vines, teeming exhaustless, pleach'd / New growth about each shell and pendent lyre; [...]}}

